Credentials that slip through TruffleHog detection fall into these categories: hex-encoded credentials bypass the decoder layer entirely, double base64 encoding hides credentials behind an undecoded outer layer, low entropy credentials with repeating patterns fail the Shannon entropy threshold, and credentials containing common programming words like example or test get filtered as false positives.

Credentials that get caught include plaintext high-entropy credentials, single-layer base64 encoded credentials which the decoder processes successfully, and credentials with sufficient randomness that pass both entropy thresholds without containing false positive terms.

The boundary behavior at exactly the threshold values shows the secret key threshold is particularly tight. A secret with entropy 4.30 like ABCDEFGHabcdefgh1234ABCDEFGHabcdefgh12 just barely passes the 4.25 threshold, while slight reductions in character variety would push it below. The ID threshold at 3.0 is more forgiving, allowing moderately patterned strings as long as they maintain some character diversity.

Detection is deterministic because all these checks are based on the content itself. The same file with the same credentials will always produce the same detection result. The inconsistency across different files with apparently similar credentials stems from subtle differences in entropy values or the presence of false positive trigger words that may not be immediately obvious.

When terminal windows in kitty are created, resized, and closed in quick succession, the system maintains consistent internal state through global state structures and nested iteration macros in state.c. The WITH_OS_WINDOW macro provides safe iteration patterns.

```c
#define WITH_OS_WINDOW(os_window_id) \
    for (size_t o = 0; o < global_state.num_os_windows; o++) { \
        OSWindow *os_window = global_state.os_windows + o; \
        if (os_window->id == os_window_id) {
#define END_WITH_OS_WINDOW break; }}
```

Window creation involves multiple initialization steps. The add_window function in state.c allocates space in the tab's window array, creates GPU resources, and initializes the window structure.

```c
static id_type
add_window(id_type os_window_id, id_type tab_id, PyObject *title) {
    WITH_TAB(os_window_id, tab_id);
        ensure_space_for(tab, windows, Window, tab->num_windows + 1, capacity, 1, true);
        make_os_window_context_current(osw);
        zero_at_i(tab->windows, tab->num_windows);
        initialize_window(tab->windows + tab->num_windows, title, true);
        return tab->windows[tab->num_windows++].id;
    END_WITH_TAB;
    return 0;
}
```

Resize events trigger signal propagation through the system. The pty_resize function in child_monitor.c uses ioctl to send resize information to the child process's PTY.

```c
static bool
pty_resize(int fd, struct winsize *dim) {
    while(true) {
        if (ioctl(fd, TIOCSWINSZ, dim) == -1) {
            if (errno == EINTR) continue;
            if (errno != EBADF && errno != ENOTTY) {
                log_error("Failed to resize tty associated with fd: %d with error: %s", fd, strerror(errno));
                return false;
            }
        }
        break;
    }
    return true;
}
```

The signal handling in child_monitor.c shows how the system processes signals from child processes. The handle_signal callback categorizes signals and sets flags for later processing.

```c
static bool
handle_signal(const siginfo_t *siginfo, void *data) {
    SignalSet *ss = data;
    switch(siginfo->si_signo) {
        case SIGINT:
        case SIGTERM:
        case SIGHUP:
            ss->kill_signal = true;
            break;
        case SIGCHLD:
            ss->child_died = true;
            break;
        case SIGUSR1:
            ss->reload_config = true;
            break;
    }
    return true;
}
```

When a window disappears before pending reactions complete, the system handles orphaned state through deferred removal. The mark_child_for_close function sets a needs_removal flag rather than immediately removing the child.

```c
static bool
mark_child_for_close(ChildMonitor *self, id_type window_id) {
    bool found = false;
    children_mutex(lock);
    for (size_t i = 0; i < self->count; i++) {
        if (children[i].id == window_id) {
            children[i].needs_removal = true;
            found = true;
            break;
        }
    }
    children_mutex(unlock);
    wakeup_io_loop(self, false);
    return found;
}
```

Live resize handling involves debouncing to prevent excessive reflows. The process_pending_resizes function uses timing thresholds to batch resize events.

```c
static void
process_pending_resizes(monotonic_t now) {
    global_state.has_pending_resizes = false;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < global_state.num_os_windows; i++) {
        OSWindow *w = global_state.os_windows + i;
